<section id="attention-is-the-scarce-resource">
<span class="ls-essay-section-number">05</span>
<h2>
<span class="ls-stroke">A larger life needs quieter tools</span>
</h2>
<p>Gallup’s July 2026 workplace report found that, among US employees using AI, 51% used it for writing and editing, 49% for search or research, and 16% for automation. These are reported uses, not measured productivity gains. They show how familiar the first forms of assistance have become. <a href="https://www.gallup.com/workplace/712736/organizational-adoption-jumps-six-points.aspx" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Gallup</a>
</p>
<p>I expect recurring work to become more accessible too. The question is what it will ask of us. Twenty overnight updates can become twenty new obligations. A tool that keeps finding work for its owner may leave the day feeling smaller.</p>
<p>The unit of progress should be a responsibility maintained well. Can I return to the research brief without reconstructing it? Is the operating review easier to question? Has the method earned enough confidence that I spend less time supervising each routine?</p>
<p>Sometimes the best result is no change. A source check finds nothing new. A draft is withheld because a number could not be traced. A job stops and explains what it needs. Restraint belongs inside the definition of useful work.</p>
<p>Flow’s Ideas board brings possible next steps beside the document; the person chooses what to pursue. Agency carries the chosen work through scope, route, review, and a recorded result. Suggestions help when they make the next worthwhile decision easier.</p>
<p>The attention returned need not become another task. It can become reading outside your field, a conversation, a walk, or time with somebody you love. A broader working life should leave room for the rest of life too.</p><PaperCta source="essay-interstitial" layout="article" class="ls-paper-cta ls-paper-cta-interstitial" eyebrow="PUT THE IDEA TO WORK" /></section>
